   133	- Value influence: program branches, the stored largest integer, and the
   134	  summaries.
   135	- Dependents: `prime-loop`, `scan-loop`, and `target`.
   136	- Validation: fixed MPY execution remains responsible for expression
   137	  evaluation; the full target closes only with `allInts`. Concrete MPY and
   138	  CPython evidence agree, and both wrong-result probes are rejected.
   139	
   140	### `primeTail` and `isPrime`
   141	
   142	- Class: definitional summaries.
   143	- Semantic role: mathematical primality specification and the exact loop
   144	  summary.
   145	- Domain: all integer pairs. `D < 2` is the unused totalization case. For
   146	  `D >= 2`, the base `D >= N` and step `D < N` cases are exhaustive and
   147	  disjoint.
   148	- Matched context: mathematical terms only.
   149	- State footprint: none.
   150	- Value influence: the candidate branch and selected maximum.
   151	- Value justification: on the step domain, the concrete equation is
   152	  `not (N % D == 0) and primeTail(N,D+1)`. The divisor-zero simplification and
   153	  the symbolic fold toward `D-1` are the two corresponding orientations of
   154	  this same equation. The symbolic fold is marked `symbolic`, while the
   155	  forward evaluator is `concrete`, avoiding a ground rewrite cycle.
   156	- Dependents: `SPEC.prime-loop`, `selectPrime`, `largestPrime`,
   157	  `SPEC.scan-loop`, and `SPEC.target`.
   158	- Validation: `SPEC.prime-loop` executes the real source loop and prints
